# Line endings are pinned because they are load-bearing here, not cosmetic.
#
# scripts/migrations/*.sql are hashed into servana.schema_migrations, and
# run-migrations.ts refuses to proceed when an applied migration's recorded
# checksum no longer matches the file. When the byte sequence depends on who
# checked the file out, that check fails for everyone except the machine that
# wrote the ledger row — which is exactly what happened before d4b0150.
#
# migrationChecksum() now normalises line endings before hashing, so the hash
# itself is already CRLF-safe and these rules cannot change any checksum.
# They exist so that the NEXT byte-sensitive gate does not have to rediscover
# this the expensive way.

*.sql text eol=lf
*.ts  text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST be LF. A hook or script checked out with CRLF fails on any
# Linux host with `bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M` — including the self-hosted deploy
# runner. Git warned "LF will be replaced by CRLF" when scripts/hooks/pre-push
# was first added, which is exactly that bug waiting to happen.
scripts/hooks/*  text eol=lf
*.sh             text eol=lf
